    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>The exercises for knee pain with the strongest evidence are quadriceps-specific strengthening, done on its own rather than inside a do-everything routine.</strong> Across 48 randomised trials, quadriceps-specific programmes scored 0.85 for pain relief against 0.39 for general lower-limb exercise, and single-aim programmes scored 0.61 against 0.16 for mixed ones.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pick one target, not five:</strong> single-type programmes beat mixed programmes 0.61 to 0.16 for pain (<em>Arthritis &amp; Rheumatology</em>, 2014).</li>
        <li><strong>Three sessions a week is the threshold:</strong> three or more weekly sessions scored 0.68 against 0.41 for fewer, with at least 12 supervised sessions.</li>
        <li><strong>Walking counts:</strong> adults over 50 with knee osteoarthritis who walked for exercise had 40% lower odds of new frequent knee pain (OR 0.60, 95% CI 0.40-0.80).</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> supervised programmes outperformed home programmes for pain and disability across 10 studies and 917 patients, so a home routine is the fallback, not the ideal.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

