      <h2 id="does-it-work">Do exercises for knee pain actually work?</h2>
      <p>Yes, and the effect is in the same range as the tablets most people reach for first. A network meta-analysis of 152 randomised trials in knee or hip osteoarthritis found exercise therapy produced similar effects on pain and function to oral anti-inflammatories and paracetamol at short, medium and long-term follow-up. An earlier analysis of trials pooled from Cochrane reviews put exercise at 0.46 and analgesics at 0.41 for knee pain, a difference of 0.06. This is why the 2022 NICE osteoarthritis guideline (NG226) names therapeutic exercise a core treatment to be offered to everyone with osteoarthritis, with medication positioned after it rather than before.</p>
      <p>One caveat belongs here, because it is the honest reading. A 2023 review argued the two numbers are not strictly comparable: the drug trials measured against placebo, while the exercise trials measured against usual care, education or another physiotherapy arm. "As good as a painkiller" is a fair summary of the direction of the evidence, not a result from a head-to-head trial.</p>

