      <h2 id="which-exercises">Which exercises for knee pain work best?</h2>
      <p>Quadriceps-specific strengthening, and the discipline of not adding anything else to it. The 2014 meta-regression by Juhl and colleagues in <em>Arthritis &amp; Rheumatology</em> is still the clearest answer, because it did not only ask whether exercise works. It asked which kind, at what dose, across 48 trials.</p>
      <p>Three of its findings matter when you are deciding what to do on a Tuesday evening. Programmes aimed at the quadriceps alone scored 0.85 for pain against 0.39 for general lower-limb exercise (p=0.005). Single-aim programmes scored 0.61 against 0.16 for programmes mixing exercise types (p&lt;0.001). And across the broad categories, aerobic (0.67), resistance (0.62) and performance exercise (0.48) were statistically indistinguishable (p=0.733).</p>
      <p>Together that says something counter-intuitive: the category matters less than the focus. A programme that does one thing properly beats one that does six things lightly, which is precisely what most knee pain listicles prescribe.</p>

