      <p>All six figures come from that one analysis, so they are comparable with each other. Two newer reviews agree. A 2024 network meta-analysis of 41 studies and 2,251 participants in <em>Frontiers in Medicine</em> ranked isokinetic strengthening highest and mixed strengthening lowest on every outcome, while noting every included trial carried a high risk of performance bias, because you cannot blind someone to whether they exercised. A 2020 review in the <em>British Journal of Sports Medicine</em> found adding resistance hip exercises to quadriceps work gave further benefit for patient-reported function; its pooled pain figure could not be read in this run, as the journal host is blocked by our network policy, so it is reported as a direction and not a number.</p>
      <p>Isokinetic machines are a clinic item, not a home one. The translation that matters is that controlled, measured resistance through a range of motion beats doing a little of everything.</p>

      <h2 id="routine">A home routine, in the order to build it</h2>
      <p>Start with the movements that load the quadriceps while moving the knee least, then add range, then add load. The repetition counts below are the ones on NHS and Versus Arthritis patient sheets, and they are clinical convention rather than numbers from a dose-finding trial.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Static quadriceps contraction.</strong> Sit or lie with the leg straight, press the back of the knee down, hold the thigh tight. Almost no knee movement, which makes it the entry point when the joint is irritable.</li>
        <li><strong>Seated straight leg raise.</strong> Sit upright in a chair with a straight back, straighten one leg and lift it. Versus Arthritis gives 10 repetitions on each leg.</li>
        <li><strong>Lying straight leg raise.</strong> The NHS Sussex Community sheet uses 3 to 5 holds of about 30 seconds.</li>
        <li><strong>Sit-to-stand.</strong> Stand up from a chair and sit back down without using your hands. An NHS primary care network sheet suggests 10 repetitions. This is the single most transferable exercise on the list, because it is the movement that fails first.</li>
        <li><strong>Step-ups.</strong> Step up with one leg leading, 10 times, then lead with the other. Versus Arthritis progresses this by using a higher step, not by adding speed.</li>
        <li><strong>Hip abduction with a band.</strong> Side leg raises, standing or lying, against an elastic band. This is the hip add-on from the 2020 BJSM review; weights or bands were the versions with evidence behind them, and multimodal add-ons were not.</li>
        <li><strong>Walking.</strong> Not a strengthening exercise, but the aerobic arm, and the one with observational evidence attached to it specifically in knee osteoarthritis.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Build to steps 1 to 5 three times a week before adding the band work. If pain is the limiting factor rather than strength, see <a href="/pages/supplements-for-knee-pain">supplements for knee pain</a> for what the evidence does and does not support alongside this.</p>

      <h2 id="dose">How often, and when will it start helping?</h2>
      <p>Three sessions a week, for at least twelve supervised sessions, is the dose the evidence points to. In the 2014 meta-regression, pain relief was larger with three or more sessions a week than with fewer (0.68 against 0.41), and aerobic exercise showed a clear dose-response: the more supervised sessions, the greater the relief.</p>
      <p>On timing, no analysis gives one number, because trials report at the follow-up points they chose rather than at the point pain first moved. Individual trials give a rough shape. In a 24-week supervised outdoor walking programme, pre-exercise pain fell by 1.20 points on a numerical rating scale, with the largest per-class improvements estimated during the first 8 weeks; a six-week aquatic programme run three times weekly reported a 69% fall in weekly average pain scores.</p>
      <p>So: expect the first change inside the first two months, not the first two weeks, and expect it to keep accruing after that. If you are taking a collagen supplement alongside and wondering which of the two is working, <a href="/pages/how-long-does-collagen-take-to-work">how long collagen takes to work</a> covers that side of it.</p>

      <h2 id="home-vs-physio">Can you do this at home without a physiotherapist?</h2>
      <p>You can, and it helps, but it is the second-best option and the evidence says so. A systematic review and meta-analysis of 10 studies and 917 patients found supervised programmes reduced pain and disability significantly more than home-based ones; within the home programmes there was a dose-response, so more frequent and longer sessions did better, and the authors concluded an optimised home programme is still clinically useful. Against that, an earlier Cochrane review found home programmes produced moderate benefits that did not differ significantly from supervised individual or class-based programmes. Both readings are in the literature; the difference probably comes from which trials were included and how loosely "home-based" was defined.</p>
      <p>The mechanism of the gap is mostly adherence, not biology. In a secondary analysis of a randomised trial in hip and knee osteoarthritis, the odds of adhering were 4.2 times higher when a physiotherapist supervised the exercise (95% CI 1.5 to 12.4, p=0.008), and an app-based alternative did not improve adherence over supervision; that study had only 68 patients, so treat the size loosely. Separate reviews found that home programmes with tracking beat untracked ones, and that occasional booster sessions with a physiotherapist helped people keep going. So: do it at home if a clinic is not realistic, but write it down, and book a review every few weeks rather than never.</p>

      <h2 id="how-much-pain">How much knee pain during exercise is acceptable?</h2>
      <p>Up to about 5 out of 10 during the exercise, provided it has settled by the next morning. NHS Scotland's osteoarthritis exercise guidance uses a 0 to 10 scale, labels 4 to 5 acceptable and 6 to 10 excessive, and says the pain should ease quickly and be no worse the morning after. The GLA:D programme's patient diary takes the same line from the other side: a score above 5 usually means the exercise should be adjusted with a physiotherapist, and increased pain lasting longer than 24 hours is a warning sign.</p>
      <p>NICE makes the same point in its guideline text, advising that people be told joint pain may increase at first and that exercise is still beneficial. That warning exists because the first two weeks are when most people quietly stop.</p>

      <h2 id="avoid">Should you stop walking, squatting and stairs?</h2>
      <p>No on walking, and a qualified answer on the rest. In an Osteoarthritis Initiative analysis of 1,212 adults aged 50 and over with knee osteoarthritis, those who walked for exercise had 40% lower odds of new frequent knee pain (OR 0.60, 95% CI 0.40-0.80), with a weaker signal for less medial joint space narrowing (OR 0.8, 95% CI 0.6-1.0). It is observational, so it shows association rather than cause, and the authors framed the advice as applying particularly to people without daily knee pain.</p>
      <p>Squatting is where the association is real, and it matters more in India than the global literature lets on. A meta-analysis of 51 studies in <em>Osteoarthritis and Cartilage</em> (2011) put occupational squatting at an odds ratio of 1.40 (95% CI 1.21-1.61) and kneeling at 1.30 (95% CI 1.03-1.63). A dose-response meta-analysis of 12 case-control studies found 1.70 times the odds (95% CI 1.35-2.13) in workers reporting kneeling or squatting, while flagging a significant risk of publication bias.</p>
      <p>Two qualifications. All of this is observational and much of it cross-sectional, so it describes an association with years of prolonged daily loading, not proof that one controlled squat damages a knee. And a community-based study that separated these activities found prolonged squatting remained a risk factor after adjusting for age, sex and BMI, while <em>sitting on the floor</em>, prolonged standing and walking up or downhill were not. For an Indian household that distinction matters: sitting cross-legged is not the exposure the studies implicate; hours a day in a deep squat, on a painful knee, is closer to it. Body weight amplifies that risk.</p>
