      <h2 id="supplements">Where a supplement fits, honestly</h2>
      <p>Behind the exercise, not instead of it. No randomised trial has tested Skelecoll's formula against an exercise programme for knee pain, and we would not expect a supplement to win that comparison if one did. NICE puts therapeutic exercise and weight management first, and nothing in a bottle changes the ranking in the table above.</p>
      <p>The exercise-before-supplement order is not special to knees: <a href="/pages/plantar-fasciitis">plantar fasciitis</a> has the same answer, with a stretching and loading programme ahead of anything sold for heel pain.</p>
      <p>What a supplement can reasonably do is support the inputs the programme depends on: protein for the muscle you are asking to adapt, and the co-factors bone and connective tissue use. On the ingredient people actually buy for knees, the 300mg of glucosamine HCl in Skelecoll is a fraction of the 1,500mg used in the major glucosamine trials, which found it no better than placebo anyway. It is a supporting co-factor dose, not a therapeutic one, and <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a> sets out what the collagen evidence does and does not show.</p>

