      <h2 id="doctor">When knee pain needs a doctor</h2>
      <p>Some knee pain is not an exercise problem, and loading it wastes time that matters. Get the knee looked at the same day if it is hot, red and very swollen with fever or chills, especially without an injury; if it swelled within minutes of an injury; if it looks deformed; or if you cannot bear weight on it at all.</p>
      <p>See a doctor promptly, rather than starting a routine, if the knee locks so you cannot straighten it, if it gives way, if there is numbness or tingling below the knee, or if pain consistently wakes you from sleep. Swelling lasting beyond a few days, or pain not improving over one to two months of sensible self-care, deserves an examination rather than another set of leg raises.</p>
      <p>If the noise rather than the pain is what worries you, <a href="/pages/knee-cracking-popping">knee cracking and popping</a> covers what it does and does not mean.</p>

