      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the single best exercise for knee pain?</summary>
          <p>If you only do one thing, do a sit-to-stand from a chair without using your hands, because it loads the quadriceps and it is the movement that fails first in daily life. The broader evidence points to quadriceps-specific work rather than any one exercise: across 48 randomised trials, quadriceps-specific programmes scored 0.85 for pain against 0.39 for general lower-limb exercise. The repetition counts on patient sheets, usually around 10, are clinical convention rather than trial-derived doses.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I fix knee pain with exercises at home, without a physiotherapist?</summary>
          <p>You can get real benefit at home, but supervised programmes did better. A meta-analysis of 10 studies and 917 patients found supervised exercise reduced pain and disability significantly more than home-based exercise, while an earlier Cochrane review found no significant difference between them. The gap is largely adherence: the odds of sticking with the programme were 4.2 times higher under physiotherapist supervision in one 68-patient analysis.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long before exercises start reducing my knee pain?</summary>
          <p>Expect the first clear change inside the first two months rather than the first two weeks. In a 24-week supervised walking programme, pain fell by 1.20 points on a 0 to 10 scale, with the largest per-session gains estimated in the first 8 weeks. A six-week thrice-weekly aquatic programme reported a 69% fall in weekly pain scores, and a 12-week walking trial reported 37%.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is it bad to exercise when my knee already hurts?</summary>
          <p>Some pain during the exercise is expected and acceptable. NHS Scotland's guidance labels 4 to 5 out of 10 acceptable and 6 to 10 excessive, and says the pain should ease quickly and be no worse the morning after. The GLA:D patient diary treats a score above 5, or increased pain lasting more than 24 hours, as a signal to adjust the exercises with a physiotherapist. NICE advises people that joint pain may increase at first and that exercise is still beneficial.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I stop walking if I have knee osteoarthritis?</summary>
          <p>No. In an Osteoarthritis Initiative analysis of 1,212 adults aged 50 and over with knee osteoarthritis, those who walked for exercise had 40% lower odds of developing new frequent knee pain (OR 0.60, 95% CI 0.40-0.80). It is an observational cohort, so it shows association rather than cause, and the authors framed the advice as applying especially to people who do not have daily knee pain.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is sitting on the floor or squatting bad for my knees?</summary>
          <p>The evidence separates the two. A community-based study found prolonged squatting remained a risk factor for knee osteoarthritis after adjusting for age, sex and BMI, while sitting on the floor, prolonged standing and walking up or downhill were not. A meta-analysis of 51 studies put occupational squatting at an odds ratio of 1.40 (95% CI 1.21-1.61). All of this is observational and describes years of prolonged daily loading, not a single controlled squat.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do collagen or glucosamine supplements replace the exercise?</summary>
          <p>No, and nothing in the evidence suggests they could. NICE names therapeutic exercise a core treatment for osteoarthritis and places medication after it, let alone supplements. Skelecoll contains 300mg of glucosamine HCl, a fraction of the 1,500mg used in the major glucosamine trials, which found it no better than placebo; it is a supporting co-factor dose rather than a therapeutic one.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is cycling or swimming better than strength work for knee pain?</summary>
          <p>They are not better, but they are not far behind, and they are easier to tolerate. In the 2014 meta-regression, aerobic exercise scored 0.67 for pain and resistance exercise 0.62, a difference that was not statistically significant (p=0.733). Aerobic exercise also showed a dose-response, so more supervised sessions meant more relief. If a painful knee will not tolerate loaded strength work yet, a bike or a pool is a reasonable place to start.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

